[White "1. Introduction"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[Result "*"]
[PlyCount "1"]

{Please give me the full line, not only the first move! I occasionally utter this sentence when talking to my students. They tend to like a particular move and decide to play it without properly analyzing the consequences. They tend to not actively search for the opponent’s best reply against that move. This course is designed to tackle this fundamental problem. We are going to deal with the fundamentals of calculation. We will train to accurately calculate a short line, involving only 3 half-moves (plys) : You will analyze one move for yourself, one move for the best-expected reply by the opponent against your move, and finally one move for your best reply against the expected move by the opponent. Here I should clarify the term 3-ply. In sequential turn-taking games like chess, a ply refers to one turn taken by one of the players. The term ply thus stands for the term ‘turn’. What is the relationship between a ply and a move in chess? In chess terminology, one move consists of a turn by each player, thus one move consists of 2 plies. In other words, one ply is a half-move. For example, in a 30-move-long chess game, 60 plies have been made in total, 30 by White and 30 by Black. Why are we training 3-ply calculation? There are several important functions for this training:  In chess, the width of calculation is more important than its length: it is so important to generate several promising candidate moves for the very first move instead of falling in love with a particular move and analyzing it to great length. Why? First, long variation is the wrong variation: the chances of going wrong in calculating a long variation are quite significant. Second, if we do not take some time at the beginning of the analysis and generate several candidate moves, then we may easily miss a strong alternative for the very first move and waste precious time in calculating in length the first candidate move that comes to mind. We must choose among options in chess, and for that, we must generate options to choose from.   By training to calculate short variations accurately, we must inevitably recognize the opponent’s resources. Against your first move, you must actively search for the most powerful response by the opponent. And then you prepare a strong response against that reply. Many beginners have difficulties recognizing the opponent’s resources.  A typical cognitive bias is also in action here: confirmation bias. When seeking data from the world, we humans tend to ‘confirm’ our hypothesis rather than search for counterexamples that could potentially ‘refute’ our hypothesis. This translates to chess in the form of wishful thinking: while analyzing, we may wish that the opponent makes a bad reply, thus justifying our move. This is in fact a very common thought process error in chess. One way of fighting this tendency is to solve mate in 2 puzzles: in order to find a forced mate in 2, you must search for the opponent’s best reply that offers the most resistance. That’s why I added several mate in 2 puzzles to this course.  There is a famous saying in chess, that is usually attributed to Capablanca. When asked how many moves ahead can he see, he says: “I see only one move ahead, but I see the best move!” While Capablanca surely can calculate more than 1-ply into the future, this claim has very important insights: First, it shows the master’s ability to quickly find the very best move in any position. Chess expertise indeed involves building chess patterns over time, and chess masters, at a rapid speed, can understand the essence of any position and limit the potential candidate moves that fit the requirement of that particular position.  Capablanca’s claim brings also another insight: that masters do not necessarily search deeper than novices when calculating variations. This is in fact known ever since de Groot’s Ph.D. Thesis, “On Thought and Choice in Chess.” Capablanca was very well known for his “Un Petit Combinaison”, “A little combination”. He was a master in calculating short lines accurately to gain a tactical or positional advantage. Here I should also mention Carlsen’s comment on what makes Nepo so good (He said it in Lex Fridman’s podcast) . He said Nepo is great at calculating short variations accurately. Even at the highest level, that ability may differ between players. Here are the factors that make this course multi-functional:   It is a mix of tactical and positional puzzles. The problem with only tactical puzzles is that you know that a tactical solution exists. This does not simulate a real game environment where most of the time you make simple decisions that improve your position, without a winning tactic. By mixing tactical puzzles with positional ones, you need to figure out what the essence of the position is. Sometimes you need a positional mini-plan to achieve your strategic goal, sometimes you execute a tactic that wins material.  Thus, we will be training “goal-setting” in calculation, which is an extremely understudied theme. In the orientation phase, when you are just understanding the essence of the position, you are engaging in a goal-setting process: is your goal to slowly build up a strategic plan? Is it to gain a strong square for your pieces? Is it to prevent an opponent’s idea? Or is it to deliver a decisive blow? Your candidate moves must get you closer to your goal. So, I want you to take some time at the beginning of a puzzle and actively search for the essence of the position. This process will also improve your ability to generate several candidate moves that fit the position’s demands.  You will train the ability to sit on your hands and not make the first move that comes to mind. It is very important for this course that before you execute your first move, you must have calculated a 3-ply line: you must have analyzed the strongest possible reply by the opponent and your reply against that move. Beginners tend to just make the first good-looking move that comes to mind, without any proper analysis - a recipe for disaster! In this course, sometimes a good-looking move will turn out to be a mistake. Expect some trickery and check things carefully! In a real game, your opponent will surely set tricks and traps for you, so it is good training!  In summary, this course is diving deeply into the fundamentals of calculation. By starting accurately analyzing the shortest possible line in chess involving 3 half-moves, we are learning the discipline of correct analyses.  We are learning the skill of generating several candidate moves.  We are learning the skill of actively searching for the best reply from the opponent.  And we are learning the skill of understanding the essence of the position and its strategic or tactical requirements, thus simulating a real game environment. The positions in this course were selected from master games as well as the games of my own students. They involve all aspects of the game (opening, middlegame, and endgame positions) . This way we simulate a real game environment and show the general applicability of the short calculation in chess. Sometimes I added prompts with the initial position, to give some guidance to the position. But they are not strictly necessary to read before solving the puzzles. When appropriate, I show the last move by the opponent, if I want to highlight a particular idea or a threat presented by that last move. In a real game, you always pay attention to the opponent’s last move, but in puzzles, this may be neglected sometimes. This is the reason why I made this addition to some positions. This course will be helpful to a wide range of players, ranging from casual players all the way to masters. The puzzles are ordered in terms of difficulty to suit the rating groups. I am sure many masters will find plenty of the puzzles challenging too. I have also tested the course on several of my students, who are all adult improvers and club players (between 1100 – 1800 ELO) . So far, I received great feedback from them. They benefited greatly from engaging in short calculations during our training sessions. It fits nicely in a group session with a quiz format, where they need to find the entire short variation to succeed. In addition, they also train the important ability to evaluate a resulting position. The calculation should always end with an evaluation, and this course also trains the evaluation ability in two different aspects:  First, at the initial orientation phase, you must evaluate the position to understand the basic imbalances and your goals.  Second, since the line you should calculate is only 3-ply deep, evaluating the resulting position does not burden the working memory resources too much: the position you must visualize and keep in mind is not too far away from the initial position. Thus, evaluation of the resulting position becomes easier. Once you master this ability in this simpler setting, you can gradually build up with deeper analysis.} 1. Z0 *

