[Black "Roadmap"]
[Result "*"]
[PlyCount "1"]

{This course contains 8 chapters followed by 4 Final Test chapters. Please do not forget to read How to Best Study This Course before proceeding with the main chapters.  Chapter 2 summarizes the skills we will train in this course, using instructive examples. I give a short summary at the end of those positions and explain the thought process required to find the solution.  Chapter 3 is a half-theoretical chapter on “Positional Themes and Mini-Plans”. Here I introduce the thematic strategic mini-plans that will be later tested in the course. These mini-plans include creating and targeting a weakness, eliminating backward pawns, handling pawn storms, burying the enemy pieces while improving yours, keeping the enemy king in the center, handling a mobile pawn center, tying down the enemy pieces to weak pawns, etc.  Chapter 4 contains 20 Mate in 2 puzzles, which serve as a warm-up. Mate in 2 puzzles are great for training short and accurate calculation. While solving mate in 2 puzzles, we must actively search for the best response by the opponent against our first move - a very important skill that beginners tend to miss. As always, please do not make the first move unless you see the follow-up.  Chapter 5 has puzzles on “Winning Material”. It is an essential skill to find tactics that win material in chess. Most beginner games are decided by tactics that lead to a win in the material. Naturally, you will use the central tactical themes such as double attack, pin, discovered attack and you will pay attention to the loose pieces in the position. Do not forget to check for blunders and the opponent’s resources!   Chapter 6 is on “Finishing Blow”. There, you will engage in 3-ply calculations to force a resignation. For that, you will exploit the opponent’s king position and pay attention to the checkmate patterns. You will look at the forcing moves but sometimes also execute deadly quiet moves to close the game.  Chapter 7 is on “Passed Pawns”. Advanced passed pawns bring lots of dynamics and tactics, as they threaten deadly promotion. There, you will train several endgame patterns to make use of the passed pawn, using short calculations. Sometimes you will engage in elegant short calculations to generate or support a deadly passed pawn, and sometimes to remove a blockade. For the Final Test positions on passed pawns, I included a crosslink to the relevant passed pawn theme covered in this main chapter.  Chapter 8 is on “Attack”. There we will train short calculations while on the attack. We will build or finish off an attack. We will engage in 3-ply calculations to effectively execute a pawn storm, coordinate our pieces, or bring the final piece into the attack. For the Final Test positions on attacks, I included a crosslink to the relevant attacking theme covered in this main chapter.  Chapter 9 is on “Defense”. Defense is used in a very broad way in this course. It sometimes refers to finding ways of not losing material, sometimes to create a counterattack in a desperate situation, sometimes to find a stalemate defense in a dire-looking position, and sometimes to simply avoid mate by using the method of elimination. For the Final Test positions on defense, I included a crosslink to the relevant defensive theme covered in this main chapter.  The Final Test Chapters contain mixed puzzles on the themes presented in the main chapters. There are 4 Final Test Chapters in this course: 2 containing prompts ( The Final Test with Scaffold 1 & 2  and Level 3 & 4 ) and 2 not containing any prompts ( The Final Test: Level 1 & 2 and Level 3 & 4 ) . The chapters with prompts involve text at the beginning of the puzzle, where I help orient the reader to the position, without revealing the solution or giving too many hints. Now please proceed with the section How to Best Study This Course .} 1. Z0 *

