[Black "How to Best Study This Course"]
[Result "*"]
[PlyCount "1"]

{First things first: We will engage in real calculation training in this course and not merely tactical pattern recognition. So please, give yourself sufficient time in solving the positions! In order to simulate a real over-the-board game environment, you can give yourself a maximum of 15-20 minutes to figure out a solution. And as mentioned, before executing your first move, you must have prepared for your upcoming move against the opponent’s best possible reply. This course has high ambitions: it aims to improve your calculation skill in all aspects of the game, as it contains mixed puzzles including positional ones. This means we require a good instructional design with a high level of support and scaffolding, especially for the upcoming players. Below I describe the instructional design and the support it contains. Since I anticipate many readers may not be well-versed in the positional mini-plans, I made Chapter 3 more pedagogical by using the following method of instruction: This chapter on positional play includes several informational chapters where I show an instructive example of a given theme (worked-out example) . The student is expected to carefully study this instructive worked-out example and the steps leading to the correct solution. This instructive example is then followed by a different puzzle on the same theme, so the reader has a chance to immediately apply what has just been learned. In instructional design, this is called an imitation task: the solution given in the worked-out example functions like a blueprint for approaching the new task. Following this method, you will engage in analogical reasoning while solving the puzzles that follow the instructional examples. Analogical reasoning is a powerful process that facilitates learning complex material in other domains. Later in the Final Test Chapters, at the end of the position, I give a crosslink to the relevant positional theme studied in this chapter, so the reader can re-study the positional theme if he/she feels the need. This will help identify and fix the weaknesses in particular areas of positional play. I gave such crosslinks not only for the Positional Mini-Plans chapter but also for chapters on Passed Pawns , Attack , and Defense . For those 3 chapters, I included the most instructive examples highlighting the different aspects of the central theme. For example, the chapter on Attack contains different attacking themes such as executing a pawn storm, coordinating our pieces, and bringing the final piece into the attack. Similarly, the chapter on Defense highlights various themes such as active counterattack, method of elimination, and stalemate defense. I set all positions as puzzles for those 3 chapters. But if the reader needs more scaffolding and support, these 3 chapters can thus be studied as “instructional”, worked-out examples, and the lessons learned in these main chapters can later be applied in the Final Test Chapters. The Final Test Chapters contain mixed puzzles on the themes presented in the main chapters. The strength of this course is it contains positional and tactical puzzles in a mixed format, so you must figure out what type of solution the given position requires. This simulates a real game environment, where nobody tells you whether there is a tactical solution. There are 4 Final Test Chapters in this course: 2 containing prompts (The Final Test with Scaffold) and 2 not containing any prompts (The Final Test) . The chapters with prompts involve text at the beginning of the puzzle, where I help orient the reader to the position, without revealing the solution or giving too many hints. The chapters with prompts serve as a scaffold for the upcoming players who would need help with the orientation to the position. Once they gain independence, they can then tackle the final test chapters which do not involve any prompts or hints. Naturally, for all puzzles, one can turn off hints at any moment to make the prompts disappear. The puzzles in both final test chapters are ordered in terms of difficulty (from 1 to 4) . I divided each final test chapter into two chapters with different difficulties: one chapter for Level 1 & 2 positions and one chapter for Level 3 & 4 positions. In learning theory, there is a concept called “desirable difficulty”. It basically means problems that are just hard enough to solve facilitate learning as long as the effort was successful in the end. Too easy and too difficult puzzles are thus not great for learning. Thus, I recommend you stay in that sweet spot of difficulty where the positions require real effort, but you can usually find the right answer in the end. If they become too difficult and cause frustration and discouragement, then you can stop and tackle that difficulty at a later stage of your chess improvement journey. Having said that, your positional chess understanding may be better developed than your attacking play - thus you may be able to solve difficult positional puzzles while failing on moderate attacking puzzles. Thus, I welcome you to tackle puzzles across all difficulties to test your skills on the spectrum. Please use this course as a tool to give you information about your strengths and weaknesses, and areas that you should mostly focus on. As mentioned, this course may help you identify weaknesses in particular areas of the game such as positional play, attack, defense, finishing blow, etc.  But also, equally importantly, this course may help you identify broader errors in the general thought process: Here I list the most common analytical thought process errors that this course wants to tackle so that you become more deliberate in which skills you want to improve as a result of this course:   Some players tend to make the first move that comes to mind without proper analysis or calculating a line. For example, they just give a check and see what happens after that.  Some players do not spend much time in the initial phase of a puzzle. They immediately start checking the moves that come to mind. Thus, they do not set their goals properly from the initial position.  Some players do not generate options (candidate moves) to choose from for the very first move. Instead, they see one move and analyze only that move, sometimes in depth.  Some players do not look at the opponent’s resources. They do not want to figure out whether their move can be refuted by the opponent.  Some players do not properly evaluate the resulting position after their calculation. They vaguely conclude that the final position looks good for them and execute the first move. So dear reader, you can be aware of the points mentioned above when solving puzzles. If you fail on a puzzle, please ask yourself what kind of process went wrong. What kind of an analytical error was that? Awareness is the first step to the cure, and I am sure you will fix those processes as long as you identify their existence. Later, you can target those areas in your training and fill the gaps. Deliberate practice!  I wish you all the best in your chess career, dear reader. I hope this course will help you improve as a chess player. You can always send me your feedback on Chessable, I would be very happy to respond. Now, time for some short and sweet calculations!} 1. Z0 *

