{This is a nice follow-up to the previous position. Sometimes we can use tactics to serve our strategic goal. This is sometimes called Sam Shankland Rule in chess circles. It basically means if a positionally desirable move looks unplayable at first sight, we should strive to make that move work anyways! We should be stubborn and actively look for tactics to justify our positionally desirable move. Here, White's strategic goal is to eliminate the weak pawn on d3 . White uses a 3-ply calculation to solve the problem. Also notice that here Black is very close to achieving total domination with c7-c5 . This means it is a critical moment for White, and we need to do our best to avoid passivity.} 5. d4 {A timely elimination of the backward pawn! Plus, now we are putting pressure on the center. A multi-purpose move!} (5. Rd1 {would not achieve the same goal due to} c5) 5... exd4 (5... Nd7 6. Rd1) 6. e5 {The tactical justification of the previous move. Short and sweet sequence, guided by the correct strategy! In this course, you will be given a chance to execute such short tactical sequences to achieve your various strategic goals.} ({Qe6 pins the e-pawn but White can achieve a winning position with several moves, e.g.} 6. Qd3 {and the f6 -knight cannot move due to the threat on h7 .}) *

