{Can you find a strong mini-plan for Black, focusing on the pawn structure? Remember the Sam Shankland Rule?} 1... c5 {Liquidating the backward pawn on c7 , with the help of a short calculation.} ({Black could not waste much time:} 1... Re7 $2 2. d5 $1 {and White fixes the c7 -pawn as a target and gains the c6 -square. Nf3-d4-c6 plan looks strong now. White is clearly better.}) 2. dxc5 ({Black is happy after} 2. d5 {because there is no more a weakness on c7 and Black obtained a 3-2 majority on the queenside. 2... f5 is an interesting idea now, together with ... Nd7-e5 plan.}) 2... dxc5 {And the d8 -rook has now become active and is hitting the queen on d3 , thus the c5 -pawn cannot be captured.} *

