{Pinter - Timman, 1982. We make plans in chess in relation to the weaknesses in the position. We must be aware of the weak pawns for example. Here Timman executes a strong mini-plan which targets a weak white pawn.} 9... Nc8 {The main weakness is the pawn on a4 , thus Timman transfers the knight to b6 !} 10. Kd3 Nb6 {That's it! Black achieved the strategic mini-plan. The a4 -pawn cannot be saved. The rest of the game is a matter of technique as they say.} 11. h4 Nxa4 12. Bc1 Nb6 13. g4 Kf7 14. Bd2 a4 15. Bc1 Kf6 16. Ba3 g5 17. h5 Ke7 18. Bc1 Nd7 19. Ke2 Nf6 {Again, targeting the weaknesses on e4 and g4 , thus tying down the white king.} 20. Kf3 Kd7 {White resigned.} *

