[Black "Weak Pawns and Tied-Down Piec"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/3nk3/1pR5/p2p1r1p/P2P1p1P/3N1P2/1P3K2/8 w - - 0 37"]
[PlyCount "1"]

{Botvinnik - Alekhine, 1938. A very famous chess classic. I wanted to include this position as it perfectly highlights the problem of having weak pawns. White is having a decisive positional advantage here. Black has several weak pawns in this position (on b6 , d5 , f4 , and h5 ) . And the white pieces are very active as they put pressure on those weak pawns. This translates into terrible passivity for the black pieces, as they are tied down in defending those weak pawns.  The rook on f5 is probably the most passive rook Alekhine ever had in his games. The only duty for that piece is to defend the weak pawns here. And rooks hate defending weak pawns in chess. They are designed to attack the weaknesses, as the white rook on c6 .  So the big lesson from this example is that simply attacking a weak pawn can be a great thing, as long as that forces the enemy pieces in going passive. Once that happens, we can start gradually improving our position and put more pressure or create a second front.} 37. Z0 *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Positional Themes and Mini"]
[Black "Open File"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "5rk1/p4ppp/pq2p3/3pP3/3P4/1Q6/PP3PPP/5RK1 w - - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "3"]

{When a position has only a single open file, fighting for that file can have crucial positional importance. Here White is executing a 3-ply mini-plan to highlight this point.} 1. Qxb6 {Looks surprising, as White is improving Black's pawn structure. But White has a specific follow-up in mind.} (1. Qc3 $6 {This would not give White an advantage as Black will organize good play along the b-file and the queen is tied down in defending the weak pawn on d4 .} Rb8 2. Rc1 h6 3. b3 Qb4 {And Black obtains equality thanks to putting pressure on the d4 weakness.}) 1... axb6 2. Rc1 {The point! Now the rook takes over the only open file in the position, thus gaining a clear positional edge in this rook ending. The next operation will be to invade the 7th rank with the rook. Many positional mini-plans involve a fight over an open file in chess. Moreover, this position shows the transformation of an advantage: White's better pawn structure was exchanged for the c-file domination.} *

