[PlyCount "8"]

{This is a great follow-up that combines the concepts we have just learned: weak pawns, tied down pieces, and the open file. Here Botvinnik executes a very instructive 3-ply mini-plan, which revolves around weaknesses, tying down the enemy pieces, and activating one's own pieces.} 17... Nb5 {Simple chess! Black simply attacks the weak pawn on d4 , and this forces the only active white piece, the rook on d1 , to go passive.} 18. Rd1 {A sad necessity. The rook on d1 is so unhappy now.} Ra7 {Immediately taking over the vacated open file! Black achieves a winning positional advantage. As we have seen before, the biggest problem with having weak pawns is that your pieces must be tied down to defend those pawns when the enemy attacks them. Here, Black targeted the weak pawn on d4 to fight for the open file.} 19. h5 g5 20. Kg2 Ra2 {Reaching maximum activity, hitting the c2 pawn which cannot be adequately defended.} 21. Be2 {And White resigned here. Could you imagine Black would force a resignation after 4 moves from the initial position? Botvinnik made it look easy.} (21. Rd2 c3 22. Rde2 Nxd4) (21. Re2 Nc3) *

