[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "3. Positional Themes and Mini"]
[Black "Pawns Awakening Pieces"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "1k1r3r/pbp2p2/1p3p2/2bPpB1p/P1P4P/6P1/1P1B1P1K/R4R2 b - - 0 20"]
[PlyCount "3"]

{This is a very instructive position when it comes to pawn play with pieces in mind. Pawn play always connect to piece activity in chess. When you are considering a pawn move, it is always a good idea which pieces in your camp will get happy as a result, or whether you are making the enemy pieces happy as a result of that pawn move. Here, Black executes a simple mini-plan and gains a large positional advantage.} 20... c6 {Chipping away White's strong central pawn and at the same time activating the dormant b7 -bishop as well as the d8 -rook. What else can you ask from a single move?} 21. dxc6 {White can no longer hold on to the d5 -pawn.} (21. Be4 $2 cxd5 22. cxd5 Bxd5) 21... Bxc6 {Black is better due to better piece activity and coordination. Black executed this explosive pawn break, and liberated two pieces! No White piece got happy as a result of this break. Thus, it was a great strategic operation for Black.} *

