[PlyCount "9"]

{As we saw in the previous position, pawn play connects to the piece activity in chess. Sometimes we can execute a pawn move in order to create a weak square that can be used by our pieces. Especially knights would benefit greatly from the creation of such weak squares. Here Black executes this idea in a textbook fashion!} 3... g4 {Undermining and gaining the e4 -square for the knight! Such a simple and strong positional mini-plan. Now the f3 -pawn must give way control of the e4 -square. Moreover, this move fixes the white pawns on the same color as the white bishop and freezes White's kingside pawn majority.} 4. fxg4 (4. f4 Ne4 {And a monster knight was created on e4 ! Just compare this piece with the bishop on f2 . Black is winning.}) 4... fxg4 {The mini-plan succeeded! Now the black pieces have access to the strong e4 -square. Plus, the g4 -pawn is freezing two white pawns on the kingside!} 5. Kc2 Ne4 6. Be3 Nd6 {An instructive moment. The strong square on e4 may be used by the king as well. The knight is eyeing the c4 -outpost, while Black is preparing ... Kf5 - e4 maneuver.} 7. Kd3 Kf5 {And Black has a winning advantage with piece domination. ... a6 - a5 is coming, which will create an outside passed pawn. There is no way for White to hold this position. Notice the effect of the initial undermining pawn move.} *

