[Black "Reaching our Dream"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/p1n3bp/1p1p4/2pNp1p1/2P1Pp2/2NP1P2/PP4PP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 2"]
[PlyCount "3"]

{As we saw previously, knights love to have stable central outposts. And bishops hate being blocked by their own pawns. Here is an instructive follow-up. White engages in a 3-ply mini-plan to clarify the situation in terms of establishing a much better minor piece position. For those of you with big chess culture, I will only give you a single hint: a superfluous piece!} 2. Nxc7 {White's both knights want to sit on the d5 -square, so White exchanges a pair of knights and solve this conflict.} (2. a3 $6 Ne6 {And Black stops White's idea and targets the strong d4 -square for the knight. White's c3 -knight is a superfluous piece, using Dvoretsky vocabulary. The d5 -knight is getting in the way of the c3 -knight, and this is why c3 -knight is a little redundant here.}) 2... Qxc7 3. Nd5 {And White achieves a comfortable edge thanks to the strong knight vs. bad bishop position. The instructive thing about this example is we used an exchange operation to achieve our strategic goal. In this course, you will face similar situations where in order to reach your dream position, you will execute a correct exchange.} ({One sample line goes like the following from this position: Qd7} 3. a4 {with the idea of creating a weakness on the queenside.} Rab8 4. Rb1 h5 5. b4 cxb4 6. Rxb4 g4 7. Qb3 Kh7 8. a5 Qd8 9. axb6 axb6 {Nxb6 Rb7} 10. Nd5 {and White has won a clear pawn with a dominating position.}) *

