[White "3. Positional Themes and Mini"]
[Black "Opening up or Closing down?"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "6rr/1k2npp1/1p2p2p/pn1pPq2/b1pP3P/P1P3P1/2PQRPBN/1RB3K1 b - - 0 23"]
[PlyCount "4"]

23... g5 {One of the most important strategic judgments we need to make in a chess game is to decide whether we should open or close the game in a particular area of the board. It is almost always the king safety that decides this question. Let's take a look at this example. An opposite-side castling position. This calls for dynamism, energy, and an attacking mindset! With this pawn move, Black wants to start an attack on the kingside and open gateways against the white king. White uses a 3-ply calculation to reach the strategic goal on the kingside:} 24. g4 {The only way to keep the kingside closed! It is based on a 3-ply calculation!} (24. hxg5 $2 {The game Khruschiov - Psakhis, 2002 saw this extremely risky move! Black's attack is growing tremendously now!} hxg5 25. Nf1 g4 26. Ne3 Qh5 {And Black's heavy pieces started putting pressure on the white king along the h-file. Just compare this with the chance White missed initially!}) 24... Qh7 (24... Qf4 $4 {loses the queen after} 25. Qe1 {.}) 25. h5 {And the g- and h-files will remain blocked now. The only break for Black is ... f6 now, but that will weaken e6 and activate the white pieces along the e-file. White achieves equality! In this course, you will be presented with similar positions where you need to understand whether the position calls for closing or opening. And you will execute your idea, using a short calculation.} *

