{Fraser - Steinitz, 1867. We need to constantly struggle against the opponent's ideas and plans in chess. Here, White has sleeping pieces on the queenside, and they need time to get into the game. Steinitz executes a beautiful mini-plan to hinder White's queenside development.} 10... Ba6 {Activating the bishop with tempo.} 11. Re1 Bd3 {The point! Now the c1 -bishop has become a buried piece! White's queenside is frozen. Black's bishop pair is very powerful! A very strong positional idea from Steinitz. In this course, you will be given the opportunity to engage in mini-plans to bury the enemy pieces. The continuation of this game shows other brilliant ideas:} 12. c4 f5 13. b3 Bc5 14. Bb2 O-O-O 15. Bxg7 Rhg8 16. Bc3 Rd6 17. b4 Rxg2+ 18. Kxg2 Rg6+ 19. Kh1 Bxf2 {Coordination/development/bishop pair trumps over material.} 20. Na3 e3 {...Be4 is a huge threat now.} 21. h4 e2 22. Be5 Rg4 23. Kh2 f4 24. Nc2 f3 25. Bf6 Bxc2 {White resigned. A gem of a game!} *

