{We want to control the center in the opening, and in this game, Black is showing us why central control is very important.} 1. e4 c5 2. c3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. cxd4 {Thus, White achieves an ideal pawn center with e4 - and d4 -pawns. They are also mobile in this position as there is no Black pawn blocking them.} Nf6 {Black does not place any pawns in the center but just gets the knights out. What is the problem with this approach?} 5. d5 {The mobile pawn center rolls forward, displacing the black knights.} Ne5 6. f4 {There is no safe home for the knights. This move intensifies White's space advantage.} Ng6 7. e5 {And White achieves a winning positional advantage, as the black knights keep getting pushed around, giving White a large central domination at the same time. White's mobile pawn center causes the black army to undevelop.} Ne4 8. Qd4 {The knight is so vulnerable in the center. White achieves a winning advantage already.} ({Qa5+} 8. b4 {and White wins. In this course, we will engage in mini-plans to either build a strong pawn center or destroy the strong center of the opponent.}) *

