{Morphy - Morphy, 1849. Young Paul Morphy against his father, Alonzo Morphy. The father, having an extra pawn, wants to castle long next move and safeguard his king. What did Paul do?} 12. Nc3 {A developing move with tempo.} Qd7 13. d5 {Very important and timely! Black was ready to castle long here. This principled move makes sure that the black king will remain in the center. White thus uses the lead in development to force a big concession.} Bxd5 14. Nxd5 Qxd5 15. Bb5+ Qxb5 16. Re1+ Ne7 17. Rb1 {Morphy wants to activate all the pieces.} Qa6 18. Rxe7+ Kf8 19. Qd5 {A beautiful centralization. The end is near.} Qc4 20. Rxf7+ {A cute 3-ply calculation which leads to mate.} Kg8 (20... Ke8 21. Qd7#) 21. Rf8# {Double check, and mate! A very Morphyesque game!} *

