[PlyCount "11"]

{Korchnoi - Karpov, 1972. A stock standard pawn storm position on the board with kings having castled on opposite flanks. Can you spot a short sequence for White that achieves the strategic goal?} 17. g5 {It is a pawn storm position with opposite-side castling, thus it is all about speed and opening up lines around the opposing king!} (17. h4 $6 {This would allow a padlock formation:} g6 $1 {Black creates a padlock pawn formation! This will make it much more difficult for White to open up the kingside now, e.g.} 18. g5 h5 {keeps it closed. Thus, we understand why the game move was much stronger.}) ({h5} 17. g5 {keeps it closed too.}) 17... h5 (17... hxg5 18. Rdg1 {And the heavy pieces are getting active on the g-file! Black's counterattack on the queenside has not even started yet.} f6 19. h4 {And suddenly, the h1 -rook is joining the attack as well. White is completely winning in this position.}) 18. g6 {Open it up please! Otherwise Black would go ... g7 - g6 and close the kingside.} fxg6 {Black cannot keep the kingside closed:} (18... f6 19. Nd4 $1 {The most elegant!} exd4 20. Qxh5 {And checkmate in inevitable!}) 19. Rhg1 {The point of the previous pawn play: Now the rook got an open lane and a fresh weakness on g6 . The g5 -square has also become available to the white knight.} Qf6 20. Ng5 {White achieves a big advantage, as his attack started and proceeded much faster than Black's counterattack on the queenside. Such pawn storm positions are all about speed and initiative!} Be7 21. Ne6 (21. h4 $1 {with the idea of Rg3 - f3 was probably stronger.}) 21... Nf8 22. Nc7 {Korchnoi chose to win the exchange and later won the game.} *

