[PlyCount "29"]

{Petrosian - Smyslov, 1961. Black's kingside is lacking enough defenders and the white bishops are slicing through the kingside. How did Petrosian improve his position here?} 18. Qa4 {The queen will enter the 'zone' on the kingside.} Rfd8 19. Qe4 {Here it is! Now this dangerous battery is formed, and Black must create weaknesses around his king as a result.} g6 20. Qg4 {Avoiding the b7 -bishop and threatening Bxg6 .} h5 21. Qh3 {4 queen moves in a row, and look what White has achieved: The queen is much closer to the black king now, and the black pawns had to move forward. This has already weakened the dark squares around the black king, and now g2 - g4 is coming, opening up the kingside by using the h5 -pawn as a hook.} f5 {Smyslov decides to stop g2 - g4 , but this leads to further weakenings, i.e. the e6 - and g6 -pawns!} (21... Kf8 22. g4 {And the lines are opening on the kingside, activating the white queen too.}) 22. Bc4 {Eyeing the fresh weakness on e6 .} Rxd1+ 23. Rxd1 Kf7 24. e4 {Opening up the position.} Qf4 25. Re1 Qg4 26. exf5 {Some accurate calculations by White.} Qxc4 27. fxg6+ Ke8 28. g7 e5 29. Qxh5+ Kd7 30. Rd1+ Bd6 31. Bxe5 Nd4 32. Nxd4 {Black resigned.} *

