[PlyCount "4"]

29... Rg7 {While attacking, we should generally seek to target the weakest point around the enemy king. What is the cleanest way to finish off the attack for White?} 30. Qh5 {The simplest. Rh8 + mate is a huge threat now and Black is helpless against it.} (30. Rxg7+ {This line also wins but it is much longer and it does not end in forced mate as in the main line:} Bxg7 31. Qf7+ Kh8 32. Qh5+ Kg8 33. Bh7+ Kh8 34. Be4+ Kg8 35. Bxd5) (30. Qh6 $4 {would be a terrible blunder as it loses the queen to} Rxg3+ 31. hxg3 Bxh6) (30. Qf7+ $4 {This unfortunately does not lead to mate:} Rxf7 31. exf7+ Bxf7) 30... Bxe4 (30... Rxh7 {Now checkmate will land on the h7 -square instead of h8 .} 31. Qxh7#) 31. Rh8# {Short and elegant! One of the most effective questions you can ask yourself while attacking is the following: What is the weakest square around the enemy king? The answer to that question usually gives you a great clue for a good move. Here White decisively attacked the weakest point around the black king, i.e. the h8 -square.} *

