[PlyCount "3"]

{Fischer - Benko, 1963. Can you see how Fischer concluded the attack here?} 19. Rf6 {A beautiful blocking sacrifice, which prevents ... f7 - f5 . h7 is the weakest point and e4 - e5 is coming next.} (19. e5 $2 f5 $1 {Black was counting on this defense. The d3 -bishop is shut down and Black offers a queen trade on e8 .} 20. Qxe8 Nxe8) 19... Bxf6 (19... dxc3 20. e5 {is the main point of the previous move.} h6 21. Rxh6+ Bxh6 22. Qxh6+ Kg8 23. Qh7#) 20. e5 {With mate to follow soon on h7 . This position nicely shows the importance of recognizing the opponent's defensive plans while you are attacking. Once you do that, surprising-looking moves like 19. Rf6 may suddenly become a good candidate! And once you notice Rf6 , it only takes a 3-ply calculation to show it is winning!} *

