{Petrosian - Botvinnik, 1963. The white bishop stands much better than the black knight, and White has a protected passer on e4 . How did Petrosian improve further?} 40. axb6 {Opening the a-file for the rook but also creating a weak pawn on b6 !} axb6 41. Ra1 {There we go! The rook now occupies the newly created open file and starts targeting the weaknesses in Black's position.} Kg7 42. Ra6 {Attacking the newly created weakness and thus tying down the enemy rook.} Rb7 43. Ra8 Kf6 44. Rc8 {Preventing ... b5 and hinting Rc6 .} Ne5 45. Ke3 Nd7 46. Rc6+ Kf7 47. e5 {Once White activates the rook to its maximum, the passed pawn starts rolling. This move also creates a square for the white king.} Nf8 48. Rf6+ Kg7 49. Ke4 b5 50. Rc6 {The rook keeps full control of the position.} Kf7 (50... c4 {This trick does not save Black:} 51. bxc4 b4 52. Bc1 b3 53. Bb2 {And the pawn is stopped while the bishop is now posted on the strong diagonal.}) 51. Rxc5 {White wins a pawn for nothing with a winning position.} Ne6 52. Rd5 Ke7 53. Be3 Rb8 54. Rd6 b4 55. Ra6 Rb5 56. Ra7+ Ke8 57. f4 Kf8 58. f5 {Black resigned. A powerful endgame display from Petrosian!} *

