{Spassky - Geller, 1965. Here you must show good technique in this winning ending. Which move by Spassky forced a resignation? It is about an overloaded piece and the weaknesses in the position.} 55. Rd4 {Black resigned. The bishop was perfectly placed as it was protecting b4 and stopping Rf4 . Now the bishop must move, and White has a great followup wherever the bishop will go.} Bc5 (55... Be5 {loses the pawn} 56. Rxb4 {.}) (55... Bf8 {leads to the mainline after} 56. Rf4 {.}) 56. Rf4 {As a result of hitting the weakness on f7 , White is forcing a rook trade and obtaining a easily winning ending with an extra exchange.} Rf6 57. Rxf6 Kxf6 {with an easily winning endgame for White. This is a nice example of how to break down a fortress-like structure. White broke the fortress by simply asking a question to the overloaded bishop that was not stable.} *

