22... f5 {An evergreen game by Botvinnik, against Capablanca. How should White react to this push of the f-pawn?} 23. exf6 (23. Nh1 $1 {Amazingly, this move is a very strong alternative. The idea is to go g2 - g4 and open up the kingside!} Nf8 24. g4 $1 Qd7 25. Ng3 a5 26. gxf5 gxf5 27. Kh1 {with Rg1 and Nh5 to follow. White has a strong attack.}) (23. h4 $2 {This plan would be a mistake as it would give time to Black to organize a blockade and secure the kingside.} Nf8 24. h5 Ne6) (23. e6 $2 {would be strategically wrong as it keeps the kingside closed and allow} Nf6 {and suddenly that poor knight is coming to the e4 -outpost.}) 23... Nxf6 24. f5 {Open up the position! Just look at the black pieces which are offside on the queenside. Plus, the white army is ready for the battle along the e - and f- files. I am leaving the rest of the game for its elegance:} Rxe1 25. Rxe1 Re8 26. Re6 {Creating a strong passer on e6 !} Rxe6 27. fxe6 Kg7 28. Qf4 Qe8 29. Qe5 Qe7 30. Ba3 {The move that makes this game very special! White removes the blockade!} Qxa3 31. Nh5+ gxh5 32. Qg5+ Kf8 33. Qxf6+ Kg8 34. e7 {The point of the combination: The passed pawn decides the game as there is no perpetual check:} Qc1+ 35. Kf2 Qc2+ 36. Kg3 Qd3+ 37. Kh4 Qe4+ 38. Kxh5 Qe2+ 39. Kh4 Qe4+ 40. g4 Qe1+ 41. Kh5 {Black resigned. See the relevant Attacking Theme.} *

