17. a4 {Spassky - Petrosian, 1966.} c4 {Setting up the next move, which will keep the queenside closed.} (17... a6 18. bxc5 Nxc5 19. Rb1 {And White will put pressure along the b-file.}) 18. Be2 a6 {The point of the previous move. Now the padlock is formed on the queenside pawns and White won't be able to open up lines. The black king is safe, while the white king will be bombarded soon along the g-file!} 19. Kh1 (19. b5 a5 $1) (19. a5 b5 $1) 19... Rdg8 20. Rg1 Rg4 21. Qd2 Rhg8 22. a5 b5 {Now the queenside is completely closed, Black switches to the actions on the kingside. Petrosian converts skillfully:} 23. Rad1 Bf8 24. Nh2 Nxe5 25. Nxg4 hxg4 26. e4 Bd6 27. Qe3 Nd7 28. Bxd6 Qxd6 29. Rd4 e5 30. Rd2 f5 31. exd5 f4 32. Qe4 Nf6 33. Qf5+ Kb8 34. f3 Bc8 35. Qb1 g3 36. Re1 h3 37. Bf1 Rh8 38. gxh3 Bxh3 39. Kg1 Bxf1 40. Kxf1 e4 41. Qd1 Ng4 42. fxg4 f3 43. Rg2 fxg2+ {One of the most beautiful games of all World Championship matches! (See the relevant Positional Theme. )} *

